#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/id #uuid "b7cba6bc-39b5-5e03-ba33-c1fc2a1b4ae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21735" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4x(0) & =0 \\\\ 5x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)(1-0.2(2))-(4)(2)y & =0 \\\\ 10(1-0.4)-8y & =0 \\\\ 10(0.6)-8y & =0 \\\\ 6-8y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2, 0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n(5, 0), \\qquad\n(2, 0.75).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4936474f-5e5f-5da2-8242-1b48336a836c" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Stable Case"}] :question/id #uuid "3e280ec5-c95a-5ca6-ba2e-edc4cf653ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21736" :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=5x(1-x)-xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-x)-xy)=5-10x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/52/52a848e561f814865aa10f5196bdf756a7f3a83a65e2cb5442f6df17709f2a1d.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/cb/cbdbf12c92fcce1f2fedcf11402643b8bbe75bf08b48de3f0d36a0a0bb814d2d.svg) to get\n\n![](images/29/29b4d10f386df1ad331ef73a88986089cf90d566128ee8abc69dcad53392ed3b.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-5(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2.5+\\lambda )+2.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2.5\\lambda +2.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = -1.25 \\pm 0.25\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable spiral sink."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Predator Extinction Case"}] :question/id #uuid "3076e962-385d-5c19-8179-7c1a91aa6e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-21738" :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-3xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=4-16x-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/ef/ef96765bdead0580d208e3a7303b9f74443a1154df877b6f00b085c7a2a60f07.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/62/6282a23eb5bd51c399548652efe32b63b2b69b661776c99b2aa2168a2d11366c.svg) to get\n\n![](images/90/902cae135690e7001927c07cfd15e72805cb65d7ba645659e0c30f19e2016226.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -4$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable sink."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/id #uuid "c8159388-bbcc-5204-8ed3-e9c2b9157d59" :question/math-academy-id "e-21739" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x) - 3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $6x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y = 0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480324} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1969079-3801-5540-ad52-a2686eb7dcd2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6036bb50-cd4e-590c-9c42-05fdc24940b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335563" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $1.5x(1-0.5x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-0.8xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5x(1-0.5x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :k