 this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.8x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71bba99b-2878-5cf7-939d-3472d3c673da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71bba99b-2878-5cf7-939d-3472d3c673da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370385} :answer-field/id #uuid "dafac934-f53f-573e-9679-5dd5e6863862" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7833a450-0184-58b0-b600-65ac5258889b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335566" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.2x(1-0.05x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-1.6xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.2x(1-0.05x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=20.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480501} :answer-field/id #uuid "c17b32bc-6591-55dc-9066-69fac3398247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da4c8dfe-99e9-5427-8a0d-f6ac8ce055dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335573" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+2.8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.6x(1-0.02x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-2.2xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.6x(1-0.02x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=50.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $50.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef1c826e-d7ac-5e00-95ab-6753058f73d2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity for the Prey"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef00333a-9645-565b-82d3-8f42ebfe86b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef00333a-9645-565b-82d3-8f42ebfe86b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370391} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d164ef-2161-59b8-a136-7f7f4a1865ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e585eab-4fdd-53d8-83e1-a034834d3c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335574" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $2x(1-0.2x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=5.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480238} :answer-field/id #uuid "18a49556-8fde-5c11-8125-ba135426b69a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480250} :answer-field/id #uuid "353326f8-9acc-57c1-af8e-a5a60d3c82e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480147} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a65ede-be2c-5b54-bdde-4d97adb3efd9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc2a604f-ae58-5276-9fe8-1d80b5831936" :question/math-academy-id "q-335579" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 2.4-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.2).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95c6bb5-0bea-53d7-8912-03fb824348e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d95c6bb5-0bea-53d7-8912-03fb824348e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370397} :answer-field/id #uuid "926c62d3-33b1-5a68-9893-0017786e7108" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f999bb63-f180-5d3e-bfde-e9b5319a01a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f999bb63-f180-5d3e-bfde-e9b5319a01a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370399} :answer-field/id #uuid "919a3769-36fa-5a1a-8af2-ab2341d81379" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eef3c976-4bf6-5772-a2a0-3b1d04bedb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef3c976-4bf6-5772-a2a0-3b1d04bedb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370401} :answer-field/id #uuid "038665d6-3284-5218-82a4-c5a18c2023dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e7ec761-7feb-53eb-a7d4-70a4387f7a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-335586" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.5x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)(1-0.5(1))-(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1-y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480346} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba92e4a-e5e2-517a-a37c-5e3d9f7d7c95" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480547} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480381} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0e2165-d2ff-5713-97bf-c955e40a3141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480498} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6eed6df-13ab-5453-bace-370fffcaabbf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f820e7d-e65e-5149-80bc-fca1cd9160a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335587" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.8).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.8).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9d18baa-dd05-52e3-948e-e347be54cd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9d18baa-dd05-52e3-948e-e347be54cd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370404} :answer-field/id #uuid "12432354-1310-5714-b859-2033e8bc3468" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370403} {:answer-field/