xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.25x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.25(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(2,1.5).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444e892-e49f-5ea7-9ac9-c51078566c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Modified Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480431} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f12926-e806-5294-840b-8aafdee3fa78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/re