1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480206} {:answer/id #uuid "a8625f49-7424-50c0-9f62-a725f5bc506e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480396} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3e6485-cbb3-5542-a015-44b4455faf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480398} {:answer/id #uuid "bf250fae-4598-5cc9-89c0-fae9effe9e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=-1\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8625f49-7424-50c0-9f62-a725f5bc506e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0b6904-a5d4-574c-9a33-7bc8ba936b06" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d03c10d6-8d9d-5373-9791-44914057d978" :question/math-academy-id "q-335654" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=8x(1-0.75x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8x(1-0.75x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-4y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8x(1-0.75x)-xy)=8-12x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8x(1-0.75x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y+4xy)=-4+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}8-12x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -4+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}8-12(1)-(2) & -(1) \\\\ 4(2) & -4+4(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 8 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-8(-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since both eigenvalues are real and negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying 