able" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480202} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7088ae-784d-51c1-93df-1fea7c2242df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a948036-666f-5d7d-922d-48953f4d4d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480130} :answer-field/id #uuid "faf226ee-4861-5e14-8b25-2eece69e5941" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2da83aef-8248-5784-87a2-78e988b82e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-335677" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=3x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x(1-2x)-xy)=3-12x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+4xy)=-3+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}3-12x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -3+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}3-12(0.5)-0 & -0.5 \\\\ 4(0) & -3+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -0.5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$"}]}
